<article class="env-definition" id="02GI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02GI">Definition <span data-tag="02GI">29.37.1</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say that $f$ is <em>étale at $x \in X$</em> if there exists an affine open neighbourhood $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) = U \subset X$ of $x$ and affine open $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) = V \subset S$ with $f(U) \subset V$ such that the induced ring map $R \to A$ is étale. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $f$ is <em>étale</em> if it is étale at every point of $X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A morphism of affine schemes $f : X \to S$ is called <em>standard étale</em> if $X \to S$ is isomorphic to 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R[x]_ h/(g)) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)  \]
</div>
<p>
 where $R \to R[x]_ h/(g)$ is a standard étale ring map, see Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/00UB" data-tag="00UB">10.144.1</a>, i.e., $g$ is monic and $g'$ invertible in $R[x]_ h/(g)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>